A||||10||Standard||My version of the electric powered Fiero...||||11||
Z||000000||Standard||01-02-2001||05:51 AM||||Also titled: Where'd my alternator go?<P>Ever since it started getting cold here my alternator has been a little bit slow to start working the first time I start my car in the day, I usually have to give it a rev or two before it put's out any current.  If I leave it sitting for more than a day it takes a loooong time before it starts working, but I don't think it's going to cooperate this time.<P>I know my belt is tight, no squeeking at all back there.  <P>What happens is, I start my car up and the volt-meter sits at about 10 volts.. let the car warm up for a few minutes, rev it and (if I'm lucky) it jumps right up to it's usual 15.  I've measured the output at the battery when the alt is working, and it's right at the recommended 14.7-14.8 range.  <P>I'm guessing it's the voltage regulator, but I'm really hoping it's just a dirty/corroded/loose connection inside.  I'm pulling the alt when I wake up today.. any idea's on what to look for?<P><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 153,000 miles and some busted up ground f/x   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/redface.gif">  <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif">  <BR>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd<BR>AIM: Standard88||||reg||11
Z||000001||maninblack||01-02-2001||07:52 AM||||after all,it sounds like to me that the battery is not taking charge when it should be, maybe try replacing that 1st||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-02-2001||08:42 AM||||I trying to remember just what a friend of mine(runs an alt. repair shop) told me to do. There is  "turn-on" wire (one of the two small wires coming from the top of the alt.) that gets the alt. going. When I was doing the dash on my kitcar I didn't hook this wire up to the ignition and I had to rev the engine up a few times to get the alt. to work. I hooked it up and it worked just fine. I might see him today and if I do I'll find out exactly what one it is. He did say that it wouldn't hurt anything the way it was. So it may very well be a loose connection||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||01-02-2001||05:57 PM||||Well, didn't quite get around to pulling the alt yet.. freakin' girlfriend  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  But I know it's not the battery, which has been charged fully several times because it was the only thing providing power to my car occasionaly.  Also had to put in a different battery to get it home a few days ago... 14 miles at night in a snow storm.  Alternator never kicked in.  I think I'll go work on my car now...  ||||reg||
Z||000004||Shaft||01-02-2001||06:59 PM||||I don't know if this is the same problem or not but...<P>I just replaced the alternator on my Nissan and since they replaced it I would notice a similar problem. All the lights on the car would be real dim (like they were running off just the battery) until I would rev it up to 3k RPM's. At the magic number of 3000 everything would brighten and the car would then be fine. The mechanic told us that this was because the "exciter" in the alternator was bad. Apparently the "exciter" tell the alternator when to put out full power. They replaced my alternator again for free.<P>I hope this helps you out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tweek||01-03-2001||12:37 AM||||I thought I would mention that I started having this same problem about a week ago. Mostly annoying so far. But you can watch the gauge move when turning on anything, it even moves back and forth with the blinkers. It does require about 30 to 60 sec of idleing and a rev or to to make it power up. I have only owned it for 3 months and dont know any of the history of it. Scary considering Im planning on a drive to CT next week, 5-7 hours away. Keep me posted on what you do, I would be quite interested to know.<P>-Ted<BR>Black '84<P><BR>Edited cause of a dumb@$$ typo!<p>[This message has been edited by Tweek (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||01-03-2001||01:44 AM||||OK, so I was lazy and didn't get around to doing it... tomorrow I promise  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Tweek,<BR>My gauge moves around when I turn on anything, too, which usually means dirty grounds or a weak alternator.  My '84 did that until I went tracked down and cleaned all the grounds.  Holds pretty steady now.  Once my alternator powered up it would be fine, it's just getting it to work in the first place that's the problem...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||01-03-2001||07:38 PM||||Gawd I hate working on my car in the winter.. I got so much crap in my eyes I'm surprised I can still see...<P>Just taking a break before I get to mad and really break something.. Haven't gotten the alternator off yet, figuring out how to get that 2-wire lead off sapped most of my gumption so I had to restore my reserves with some NFS 5...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||
Z||000008||Standard||01-04-2001||02:41 AM||||Well, getting the alternator off was the really easy part.  Loosened the 13mm adjusting bolt, took off the belt, took out the 14/15mm (not sure, I had to use a 9/16" socket  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) on top, then went back under and took out the 13mm and snaked it down.  More than enough room with the stick.  I heard it was a real b!tch on the auto's..<P>Toughest part was getting off the 4 bolts that held the alt together.. using a wrench would just round them off, so I ended up cutting off the heads.  <P>How are you supposed to get a paperclip in there to hold the brushes?  I couldn't and I figured it wouldn't be much of a problem, but I ended up crushing the bottom one...<P>After taking a look at the inside, I think I'm going to go get a rebuilt one.  This must be the original alt because it is corroded beyond belief inside there...  anybody have any recommendations for good brands?  ||||reg||
Z||000009||Standard||01-05-2001||03:26 AM||||Well, went to checkers and got a rebuilt alternator.. Had to switch the pulley's over, the one I got came with a v-belt pulley.  Which meant even more grinding and drilling and just generally destroying my old one to get that freakin' big nut off the top.  <P>Then discovering that they didn't align the two halves when they rebuilt it after spending 20 minutes cursing at it because it wouldn't fit.. glad I marked my old one.  Had to rotate it 90 degree's, and it slid in just fine.  Got the belt nice and tight, plugged everything in and crossed my fingers.. started her up and the volt-meter shot right up to where it's supposed to be  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> I hope it's the last time I have to work on that for a few years...  <P>I got so much stuff in my eyes I'm suprised I can still see.. ||||reg||
Z||000010||Tweek||01-05-2001||06:02 AM||||Im thinking that I am going to have to replace my Alt soon too... which is a normal maintenence item I suppose. The one thing that is going to make this one a real pain in the arse, is that the bolt that the alt pivot on (ie holds it in there) is snapped in 2 in the middle somewhere. It's staying in there for now. But I noticed that the wheel well is about 1 inch from the end of that bolt that needs to come off. Nothing a hacksaw cant fix i suppose. =) BTW What places well swap pulleys for you? I have a alt that was supposed to go on an 87 grand am (same motor) but has a different pulley. Ive got decent mechanical skills, but limited tools.....<BR>Glad to hear that you fixed it. God forbid you have to do that again.. lol<P>-Ted<P>Black '84 Auto||||reg||1
